<h2 id="action-cameras">Action cameras</h2>
<p>Filming great video from the air is easy with the range of action cameras available. Most are waterproof and can take a lot of rough handling, even some crashes and still keep working. Attaching an action camera to an aircraft is a great way to capture still and video images from a unique perspective.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/action-camera.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/action-camera.jpg" alt="Front of a GoPro action camera: a small black box with a square lens on the right and a color screen on the left showing an underwater scene." width="1600" height="840" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>An action camera (GoPro). Small, rugged, and often waterproof, it is easy to mount on an aircraft.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="getting-good-video-from-an-aircraft">Getting good video from an aircraft</h3>
<p>An action camera will record something no matter how you mount it, but a few things make the difference between shaky, warped footage and video you are proud of:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vibration.</strong> Most small cameras use a rolling shutter: the sensor records the picture one row at a time from top to bottom instead of all at once. If the camera is shaking, each row is recorded in a slightly different place and the picture wobbles like jello. Balance your propellers and mount the camera on something soft, such as foam tape or rubber dampers, so the vibration doesn’t reach it.</li>
<li><strong>Stabilization.</strong> Many action cameras have electronic stabilization built in, which crops the picture slightly and shifts it to cancel out bumps. It works well for small movements. For the smoothest video, larger drones carry the camera on a gimbal, a motorized mount that keeps the camera level while the aircraft moves.</li>
<li><strong>Frame rate and resolution.</strong> Higher frame rates, such as 60 frames per second, make fast motion look smoother and let you slow the video down later. Higher resolutions, such as 4K, show more detail but fill your memory card faster and use more battery.</li>
<li><strong>Propellers in the picture.</strong> Because of the rolling shutter, a spinning propeller in the frame can look bent or broken into strange shapes. Mount the camera where the propellers stay out of view.</li>
<li><strong>Weight and balance.</strong> A camera is a payload like any other. Mount it securely so it can’t fall off, and check the center of gravity again after you add it. A camera on the nose can make an airplane nose-heavy, and one on the tail can make it dangerously tail-heavy.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="360-cameras">360° cameras</h2>
<p>While an action camera can capture great video, a 360 camera will capture video in every direction and will auto stabilize the image. This means that you do not need to worry about what the camera is pointing at as it will capture everything. In post-production you can select the camera views that you want.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/360-camera.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/360-camera.jpg" alt="An Insta360 camera standing upright: a tall, narrow black body with a round fisheye lens at the top and a screen below it showing a wide-angle selfie of two hikers." width="1600" height="1600" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A 360° camera (Insta360). It has a lens on each side, so together they record every direction at once.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="how-a-360-camera-works">How a 360° camera works</h3>
<p>A 360° camera has two fisheye lenses facing in opposite directions, each seeing a little more than half of the world around it. The camera’s software stitches the two pictures together into one sphere. Where the two pictures meet, called the stitch line, anything very close to the camera can look doubled or cut off. That is useful on a drone: mount the camera so the aircraft sits in the blind spot along the stitch line, and the aircraft seems to disappear from the video.</p>
<p>There is a tradeoff. A 360° camera spreads its pixels across the whole sphere, so when you pick out one normal-looking view afterward you are only using a small part of the picture. A 5.7K 360° video cropped to one direction looks noticeably softer than video from a regular action camera pointed in that direction. Use a 360° camera when you don’t know where the interesting thing will be, and a regular camera when you do.</p>
<h2 id="multispectral-cameras">Multispectral cameras</h2>
<p>If your work requires the analysis of plants or other objects that need more analysis than a basic video camera can capture, then a multispectral camera may be what you need. A multispectral camera will capture wavelengths of light outside of the visual range. This can be used to analyze crop health and stress, detect pests and crop damage and other agricultural issues.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/multispectral-camera.png"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/multispectral-camera.png" alt="A multispectral camera with a red and silver body and six lenses in a black faceplate on its front, with connectors on the side." width="675" height="450" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A multispectral camera. Each lens records a different band of light, including wavelengths outside the visible range.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="how-a-multispectral-camera-works">How a multispectral camera works</h3>
<p>A normal camera records three colors: red, green, and blue. A multispectral camera records several narrow bands of light, each through its own lens and filter. A typical agricultural camera records blue, green, red, red edge (the band where plant reflection changes quickly, between red and infrared), and near-infrared (NIR). Near-infrared is just beyond red, and our eyes can’t see it.</p>
<p>Plants make that infrared band very useful. The chlorophyll in a healthy leaf absorbs most of the red light for photosynthesis, while the structure of the leaf reflects a lot of near-infrared light. A stressed or dying plant absorbs less red and reflects less infrared. Comparing the two bands tells you how healthy the plants are, often before you could see a problem with your eyes.</p>
<p>The most common way to compare them is the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index, or NDVI:</p>
<p><strong>NDVI = (NIR − Red) ÷ (NIR + Red)</strong></p>
<p>NDVI always falls between −1 and 1. Dense, healthy vegetation scores close to 1, bare soil and rock score close to 0, and water, snow, and clouds score below 0. Mapping software colors each part of a field by its NDVI, so a farmer can see at a glance where crops are stressed and go check that spot.</p>
<p>Because the camera measures how much light the plants reflect, it also has to know how much light was shining on them. Many multispectral cameras come with a calibration panel, a target with a known reflectance that you photograph before and after the flight, and a sunlight sensor mounted on top of the aircraft that measures the incoming light during the flight. Flying in steady light, near midday and not in and out of clouds, gives the most consistent results.</p>
<p>A thermal camera is a related tool. Instead of reflected light it records the long-wave infrared heat that objects give off, which makes it useful for finding people and animals, spotting heat leaking from buildings, and checking solar panels and power lines.</p>
<h2 id="first-person-view-fpv-cameras">First person view (FPV) cameras</h2>
<p>Ever wonder what it would be like to sit in your RC aircraft and fly it? With an FPV (First Person View) camera you can! These small cameras transmit near-real-time video back to a video monitor or headset on the ground allowing you to fly the aircraft from the point of view of the airplane. The main components of an FPV system are a camera, video transmitter (VTx), a video receiver (VRx) and a video display (usually a set of goggles). FPV systems can be either analog or High Definition digital. Analog systems are simpler and lower cost but the high definition digital systems have much higher quality video.</p>
<h3 id="analog-fpv">Analog FPV</h3>
<p>Analog FPV works much like old broadcast television. The camera sends a standard analog video signal to the video transmitter, which broadcasts it on a radio channel, usually in the 5.8 GHz band. The video receiver in the goggles is tuned to the same channel and shows the picture. The 5.8 GHz band is divided into dozens of channels, grouped into bands with letters such as A, B, E, F, and R (Raceband). When several pilots fly at once, each one has to use a different channel or their pictures will interfere with each other.</p>
<p>Analog has two big advantages besides low cost. The delay between the camera seeing something and the pilot seeing it is very small, which matters when you are flying fast and close to obstacles. And when the signal gets weak, the picture doesn’t just stop: it gradually fills with static and lines. That is your warning to turn back before you lose the picture completely.</p>
<p>Video transmitters can be set to different power levels, commonly from 25 milliwatts up to several hundred. More power gives more range but makes more heat, uses more battery, and is more likely to interfere with other pilots, so use only as much as you need. Never power up a video transmitter without its antenna attached. With nowhere for the power to go, the transmitter can overheat and damage itself.</p>
<p>Check the voltages before you connect anything. Many FPV cameras run only on 5 volts, while many video transmitters accept a wider range or run straight from the flight battery. Some transmitters supply 5 V for the camera, and some don’t. Read the labels and instructions for both parts, and add a voltage regulator if needed, as the <a href="/resources/fpv-setup-lab/">FPV setup lab</a> reminds you.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/fpv-camera.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/fpv-camera.jpg" alt="A small black RunCam Phoenix 2 FPV camera with a round lens on the front, a mounting hole on the side, and a white connector on top." width="1014" height="948" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>FPV camera (RunCam Phoenix 2). A small, light camera that sends live video to the video transmitter.</p></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/video-transmitter.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/video-transmitter.jpg" alt="A small video transmitter circuit board with a short cable running to a gold antenna connector." width="700" height="700" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>FPV video transmitter (VTx). It sends the camera’s video from the aircraft to the ground; the antenna screws onto the gold connector.</p></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/fpv-goggles.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/fpv-goggles.jpg" alt="White and blue Fat Shark FPV goggles with a padded face opening, a blue head strap, and channel and volume buttons on the side." width="1560" height="1157" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>FPV video display goggles with a built-in video receiver (VRx). The pilot sees the aircraft’s live video inside the goggles.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="high-definition-fpv">High-definition FPV</h3>
<p>Digital FPV systems such as DJI, Walksnail, and HDZero send compressed high-definition video over a digital radio link, so the picture is much clearer and sharper than analog. Many can also record high-definition video on the aircraft or in the goggles, so you don’t need a separate action camera.</p>
<p>The tradeoffs are cost, size, and weight, and the camera, transmitter, and goggles usually all have to come from the same system. Some digital systems have slightly more delay than analog. They also fail differently: as the signal gets weak the picture may turn blocky, stutter, or freeze, and then drop out suddenly, with less warning than the static of an analog system. Learn what your system looks like at the edge of its range while you are still close by.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/hd-fpv-goggles.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/hd-fpv-goggles.jpg" alt="Front view of gray DJI digital FPV goggles fitted with two Lumenier AXII HD antennas on their front face and two more antennas at the top corners." width="1514" height="931" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>High-definition digital FPV goggles (DJI). Digital systems give much higher-quality video than analog.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="fpv-antennas-and-range">Antennas and range</h3>
<p>At 5.8 GHz, radio signals behave almost like light. They travel in straight lines and are blocked by trees, buildings, hills, and even your own body. Keep a clear line between the aircraft and the receiver, and mount the aircraft’s antenna where the airframe, batteries, and especially carbon fiber don’t block it.</p>
<p>Most FPV antennas are circularly polarized, meaning the radio wave twists like a corkscrew as it travels. This helps the signal cope with reflections. Circular antennas come in two types, right-hand (RHCP) and left-hand (LHCP), and the antennas on the aircraft and the goggles must match. A mismatched pair loses most of the signal. Omnidirectional antennas receive from all directions, while directional antennas such as patch antennas reach farther but only in the direction they are pointed. Many goggles use one of each.</p>
<h3 id="on-screen-display">On-screen display</h3>
<p>Many FPV systems can show flight information on top of the video, called an on-screen display (OSD). If the flight controller supports it, the OSD can show the battery voltage, altitude, speed, flight mode, GPS satellites, and an arrow pointing back home. Watching the battery voltage is especially important, because in the goggles you can’t look down at your transmitter or a battery alarm.</p>
<h3 id="fpv-rules">FPV rules: the FAA and the FCC</h3>
<p>FPV is great fun, but the rules about seeing the aircraft still apply. Under Part 107, the view through FPV goggles or on a monitor does not count as visual line of sight. When the pilot is wearing goggles, a visual observer has to keep the aircraft in sight with their own eyes and stay in constant communication with the pilot, so either of you can take action if something goes wrong. Recreational flyers need a visual observer too, standing beside the pilot. The <a href="/part107/">Part 107 course</a> covers visual line of sight and visual observers in detail.</p>
<p>The radio side has rules too. Very few FPV video transmitters, especially analog ones, are certified by the FCC for use without a license. To use most of them legally in the United States you need an amateur (ham) radio license. The entry-level Technician license covers the 5.8 GHz band and only requires passing a multiple-choice exam. Some digital systems are certified for license-free use, but sometimes only on certain channels or power levels. If a transmitter has a valid FCC ID you can look up, it is certified; if not, you need a license to use it.</p>
<h3 id="flying-fpv-safely">Flying FPV safely</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fly with a safety pilot.</strong> For your first FPV flights, fly on a buddy box with an instructor who keeps the aircraft in sight and can take control instantly, as the <a href="/resources/fpv-setup-lab/">FPV setup lab</a> requires. The ASCEND transmitters have a buddy box built in; see the <a href="/resources/buddy-box-video/">buddy box overview video</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Plan for losing the video.</strong> Know what you will do if the picture freezes or fills with static: hand control to your safety pilot, or take off the goggles and fly line of sight. If your flight controller has return to launch, set it up and know how to switch to it.</li>
<li><strong>Expect it to feel different.</strong> FPV gives you a narrower view and less sense of speed and distance than you are used to, and some people feel motion sickness at first. Start slow, high, and in a wide-open area.</li>
<li><strong>Check your channel before you power up.</strong> At a field with other pilots, make sure no one else is on your channel before you plug in, or you could knock their video out while they are flying.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="automating-cameras-with-a-raspberry-pi-computer">Automating cameras with a Raspberry Pi computer</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/raspberry-pi-zero-2-w.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/raspberry-pi-zero-2-w.jpg" alt="A Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W board with labels pointing to its 40-pin GPIO header, micro SD card slot, processor, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth chip, camera interface, mini HDMI port, micro USB data port, and micro USB power port." width="1256" height="1315" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W. Its camera interface connects a camera, and its GPIO pins can connect to an RC receiver or GPS.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>A low cost single board computer such as the Raspberry Pi pictured above can be used to control a camera. A simple interface between an RC receiver and this Pi computer could trigger the camera to take pictures at the pilot’s request. With the addition of a GPS or even a flight computer the process could be automated with geotagged pictures. Geotagged pictures could then be stitched together to form a photo mosaic.</p>
<h3 id="triggering-a-camera-from-the-flight-controller">Triggering a camera from the flight controller</h3>
<p>The flight controller can do this job too. ArduPilot can trigger a camera from one of its servo or relay outputs, or tell a camera to take a picture over a data connection. It can take a picture every time the aircraft has flown a set distance, and a mission can switch that on at the start of a survey and off at the end. Each time it triggers the camera, the flight controller records where the aircraft was and its altitude and attitude. That record is what lets you geotag the photos afterward.</p>
<p>You don’t usually have to work out the distances yourself. QGroundControl’s <strong>Survey</strong> pattern, in the same Plan view used in the QGroundControl mission example in Chapter 6, lets you draw the area you want to map, choose your camera and altitude, and then creates the back-and-forth flight lines and the camera trigger distance for you.</p>
<p>A Raspberry Pi becomes useful when you want to do more than take a picture: control a camera the flight controller doesn’t support, process the images on board, or save extra data with each photo. A Raspberry Pi camera module plugs into the camera interface with a ribbon cable, and a short Python program can take a photo whenever the Pi sees a signal from the flight controller or the RC receiver on one of its GPIO pins.</p>
<h3 id="making-maps-from-photos">Making maps from photos</h3>
<p>Turning many overlapping photos into one map is called photogrammetry. Mapping software finds the same features, such as a fence post or the corner of a building, in several photos and uses them, along with the geotags, to work out exactly where each photo was taken and how the ground is shaped. The result can be an orthomosaic, a single map-like image corrected so that everything is shown from directly above, and a 3D model of the ground and buildings.</p>
<p>For this to work the photos have to overlap a lot more than you might expect. Pix4D, a widely used mapping program, recommends at least 75 percent overlap between photos along each flight line and at least 60 percent between neighboring flight lines, and more over fields, forests, and snow, where the photos all look similar. That is why survey missions fly in tight back-and-forth lines, like mowing a lawn.</p>
<p>How much detail you get depends on the ground sample distance (GSD): how much ground each pixel covers. Flying higher covers more ground in each photo but makes each pixel cover more ground, so you see less detail. With the same camera, doubling the altitude doubles the GSD. Under Part 107 you are limited to 400 feet above the ground, so the camera’s resolution and lens matter a lot for how much detail you can get.</p>
<h2 id="artificial-intelligence-cameras">Artificial intelligence cameras</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/camera-video-systems/raspberry-pi-ai-camera.jpg"><img src="/assets/camera-video-systems/raspberry-pi-ai-camera.jpg" alt="A Raspberry Pi AI Camera: a small green circuit board with a black camera module in the middle and an orange ribbon cable running down from it." width="640" height="640" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A Raspberry Pi AI Camera. It connects to a Raspberry Pi by a ribbon cable and can run image-recognition models on the camera itself.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>Artificial intelligence is starting to move into the camera itself. The Raspberry Pi AI Camera pictured above uses a Sony IMX500 sensor that can run a small neural network on the camera chip. Instead of just sending pictures, it can send results, such as “there is a person in this part of the picture,” along with the image. Because the camera does the work, the Raspberry Pi’s processor is left free for other jobs, and it works with any Raspberry Pi that has a camera connector.</p>
<p>On a drone, a camera like this could count animals or vehicles, spot a person during a search, recognize a landing target, or take a picture only when it sees what you are looking for instead of thousands of photos you have to sort through later. As you read in Chapter 8, this kind of real-time image processing is one of the biggest ways AI is changing what drones can do.</p>
<p>AI cameras have limits. They only recognize what their model was trained to recognize, the small models that fit on a camera chip are less accurate than large ones running on a powerful computer, and they make mistakes, both missing things and seeing things that aren’t there. Treat the camera’s answer as a helpful hint, and have a person check anything important.</p>
